  "hypothesis": {
    "problem": "Across all ten transcripts the minds argue method and procedure; what the user might want, fear or be pressured by, and the fact the user has not given that would change the advice, appear in section A and in section D's single 'possible self-reflection' but almost never inside the turns. C4.4 (6.66), C4.1 (7.09) and C4.3 (7.24) are the three largest point losses, 3.6 points together; C3.5 recurs on all five questions and C3.4 on four, with invented context (a serum, cold weather, a lease, renting and dating) standing in for the unnamed fact. The two debates that did set rival readings of the user side by side inside the turns (q3 s2 T9-T10, q4 s2 T2/T6/T8) scored C4.1 9.00 and 9.30 and are among the highest totals in the set.",
    "cause_type": "prompt",
    "mechanism": "Section 5 tells the minds to address one another and to avoid turning toward the user, and then treats the user's inner life only as a restriction (possibilities not verdicts, no assigned motives) with no achievement stated. The only structural slot for reflection is section D's 'a possible self-reflection' and the only slot for the decisive unknown is sections A and E, so the worker, which over-complies and keeps the safest reading, files the user's motives and missing facts as summary material and keeps the turns to method; when a turn needs a concrete detail it supplies one instead of naming the gap. The capability reference shows no C4 or C3 cell is capability-bound (this worker leads by 3 to 4 rating points everywhere), and the worker is known to write naturally when told what a great debate achieves. Stating the achievement (inside the turns, the minds contest readings of the user's wants, fears and outside pressures, name conflicts between things the user cares about, and name rather than supply the fact that would change the advice, without settling any of it for the user) gives the worker what it responds to.",
    "proposed_change": "REWRITE the two-sentence inner-life paragraph in section 5 into one paragraph of achievement rules: the user's inner life is argued inside the turns, not only in the closing summary; one mind offers a reading of what the user might want, fear or be pushed toward by other people, early enough for the others to contest it; another mind offers a different reading of the same words and they argue which the user's text better supports and what each would change; a conflict between two things the user cares about is named plainly; a fact the user has not given that would change the advice is named with what each answer would change, instead of supplied; the readings stay possibilities and the minds do not settle them for the user. Nothing else in the prompt changes; 'The minds primarily address one another' stays as the guard against turning toward the user.",
    "expected_observations": [
      "C4.1 mean rating rises from 7.09 to about 8.2, C4.4 from 6.66 to about 7.8, C4.3 from 7.24 to about 8.2 (about +1.4 total points); card examples for these cells cite turns, not only section D, on four or more questions.",
      "C3.5 rises from 7.23 toward 8.0 and C3.4 from 7.41 toward 8.0 as the missing fact is named inside a turn instead of supplied; card limitations citing invented details (a serum, a lease, renting) become rare (about +0.3).",
      "C4.5 and C7.1 rise modestly (about +0.2 each) because the stakes become the user's rather than the method's; C3.1 and C1.3 may rise where the contested reading is the central tension.",
